NAME
    browser - a small web browser for the desktop

SYNOPSIS
    browser

DESCRIPTION
    A web browser for the VNU desktop. It fetches one address over the
    socket syscalls and shows what came back as plain text, with every
    link on the page selectable and followable. It is deliberately
    small: no HTML layout, no CSS, no cookies, no history and no TLS.
    What it shows is that a desktop program has a window, a keyboard
    and a network here, and can use all three at once.

    A page is handled rather than parsed. A tag is skipped whole, from
    its < to its >, the four entities a text page uses (&amp; &lt;
    &gt; &nbsp;) become their one character and anything else in that
    position is dropped; what is left between them is laid out as text.
    A <br> is a line break and every other tag is invisible.

    An <a href="..."> is both a run of text on the page and a link that
    can be followed: its text is shown like any other text and its
    address is remembered. A link with no address, or one whose </a>
    never arrives, shows its text and is not selectable - nothing is
    offered that is not really there.

    The focus is either in the address bar or on the page. Tab moves
    between them, Enter on a link follows it, and Esc returns to the
    bar rather than quitting, so a mistyped address is one key from
    being fixed. A second Esc, from the bar, closes the window. With no
    links on the page, Tab, Enter, Space and the arrows all go back to
    the bar, since there is then nothing to move through.

    The bar shows what is being typed and the page below shows what was
    last fetched. The two are kept apart so an address that has been
    edited but not fetched is visibly still a proposal. F5 fetches the
    last address again.

    A failed fetch does not blank the window. The reason is drawn over
    the page, which stays where it was, together with the address it is
    still showing: an address with no host in it, a name that does not
    resolve, a refused connection, a request that cannot be sent and an
    empty answer are all reported this way. With nothing loaded there
    is no page underneath, so the reason is all there is to show.

    A relative link is resolved against the address of the page it was
    found on: one beginning with / takes the scheme and host and changes
    the path, and a bare name is taken relative to the directory the
    current path names. A port that is not the default is carried into
    the resolved address.

    There is no TLS. An https address is fetched over a plain
    connection, so a server that requires TLS will refuse, and one that
    does not is noted on screen as having answered in the clear.

    The window is pre-created by the desktop and input arrives through
    vgfx_poll() like every other desktop program, which is the only way
    a windowed program is delivered keys here: read(0, ...) is the
    console's byte stream, a windowed task has none of it, and the
    kernel answers that call with nothing.

KEYS
    printable  edit the address
    Backspace  delete the character before the caret
    Delete     delete the character at the caret
    Left Right move the caret; Home End go to either end
    Tab        move between the address bar and the page
    Enter      fetch the address, or follow the selected link
    Up Down    choose a link; Home End the first or the last
    F5         fetch the last address again
    Esc        back to the address bar, and from there close the window

NOTES
    Only the first 64 KiB of an answer is kept, and only the rows that
    fit the window are drawn. A page larger than either is shown as far
    as it goes rather than refused, and a line longer than a row wraps
    onto the next one instead of being cut off.

FILES
    /apps/browser/bin   the program itself
